# ZZ Stack — the whole configuration surface.
#
#   cp .env.example .env    then fill in the required values below
#   docker compose up -d
#
# Every variable the PLATFORM reads appears in this file, commented out with the default it
# falls back to — that is what makes the sentence above true, and the gate holds it: not
# only what docker-compose.yml interpolates, which is what this paragraph used to claim, but
# every name a service or a tool reads out of the environment. The two differ, and the
# difference is where a knob goes missing: an operator reading this to find out what they
# may set takes an absent one for a knob that does not exist.
#
# The image versions are written into docker-compose.yml rather than here, because that file
# and the images it names are one release and nobody receiving it could know what to type.

# ── you must fill these ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The model provider: any OpenAI-compatible endpoint, and a key for it. The deployment's
# own model runs on Z.AI's coding endpoint, which is what PLATFORM_BASE_MODEL below names;
# point both elsewhere together, never one of them.
LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.z.ai/api/coding/paas/v4
LLM_API_KEY=

# The public address of this platform's gateway. Every client package generated by this
# deployment embeds this value, so a wrong one hands out unreachable URLs to everyone who
# installs. Must be reachable from a laptop, not just from inside the network. There is no
# default: the gateway refuses to build a package rather than guess an address for you.
GATEWAY_PUBLIC_URL=https://api.example.com

# Origins other than loopback that an MCP client may register an OAuth redirect on,
# comma-separated. Leave it EMPTY unless a hosted client connects — `https://chatgpt.com`
# for ChatGPT connectors. Native clients (Claude Code, Codex) redirect to 127.0.0.1 and are
# allowed by rule, per RFC 8252 §7.3. An origin here can receive an authorization code, which
# becomes a platform token — so each authorization for one stops on a consent page first.
OAUTH_REDIRECT_ORIGINS=

# ── you probably want to set this one ────────────────────────────────────────

# Becomes the platform superadmin: the person who can create teams, issue tokens and
# install flows. Without it nobody can administer the platform, and issue-first-pat.sh has
# nobody to mint the first token for.
SUPERADMIN_EMAIL=

# The first team, created on boot with the superadmin as its admin. SET THIS. A deployment
# with a superadmin and no team looks perfectly healthy and is not: teamFor() returns null,
# so every document lands in a per-user store instead of the team's, and nobody notices
# until they go looking for it.
BOOTSTRAP_TEAM=

# What render_agent_definition names as an agent's model. Served from LLM_BASE_URL, so the
# name has to be one that endpoint lists — `curl $LLM_BASE_URL/models` answers.
PLATFORM_BASE_MODEL=deepseek-v4.1-flash

# The deployment's own timezone. Every DATE the platform stamps is local to it — an
# initiative's name, a document's `date`, the day a source was added — because a date is what
# somebody would write at the top of a page. Timestamps stay ISO with an offset. Left as UTC
# this is wrong for the first eight hours of a Singapore working day, every day, and the
# console renders through the same zone.
ZZ_TZ=Asia/Singapore

# THE READING JUDGE, WHICH IS A FALLBACK AND NOT THE JUDGE.
#
# Every typed decision goes to the typed judgement service above: the qualitative marks, the
# thresholds, the recommendation enum. Twelve of twelve rounds since that switch carry
# `typesafe/jev-latest`. This model answers only when TYPESAFE_API_KEY is unset, or when a
# ruler carries a qualitative dimension with fewer than two named levels.
#
# IT IS NAMED ON EVERY SCORE ROW (`zz.eval.judge_model`) and every comparison groups by it, so
# changing this starts a new comparison group rather than silently re-scaling the old one.
#
# SETTING IT TO PLATFORM_BASE_MODEL IS ALLOWED AND WARNED ABOUT. A judge that IS the model the
# agents run on is exactly as good as the thing it judges, which is the one property a ruler
# must not have — but it can only do damage when the typed service is absent, so that is the
# only combination the boot log complains about. This deployment runs one model deliberately.
ZZ_JUDGE_MODEL=deepseek-v4.1-flash

# Whether the judge deliberates before answering. ON, and the control is why.
#
# It was turned off for a good reason: with it on a control call ran past 95 seconds and timed
# out three times in a row, storing nothing, and a lost control costs a round the only number
# that says the judge was reading. Off, the same call answered in five seconds.
#
# Then the control judged the change. Under the fast judge one skill's real-vs-control gap fell by a point
# and another went negative — the judge scoring the real subject above a
# different skill's text under its own ruler, which is the collapse this check exists to
# detect. So the timeout is paid instead: a timed-out subject is reported skipped, `remaining`
# does not move, and the next call retries it. A mode is part of a judge's identity, so `off`
# records as `<model>/no-reasoning` and never averages with these.
ZZ_JUDGE_THINKING=on

# The TYPED judge, and the division of labour it makes possible.
#
# The judge above READS an artifact and writes sentences about it. This one answers SHAPES and
# cannot answer outside them: a position on named levels, one option from a closed set, the
# probability that a yes/no is yes — each returned with the distribution behind it and a
# confidence that is that distribution's shape rather than the model's opinion of itself.
#
# So the two are not alternatives, they are halves. The typed service fixes the SCORE, the
# recommendation and every threshold verdict; a language model then reads the artifact beside
# those numbers and writes what they mean. A typed answer cannot be off-vocabulary, and an
# explanation written afterwards cannot invent a score to suit itself, because the score was
# settled before any prose existed.
#
# EMPTY IS SUPPORTED AND CHANGES NOTHING ELSE. With no key, a ruler whose dimensions name their
# levels simply is not marked by this judge, rulers written the old way keep the reading judge
# they were always scored by, and a report says its recommendation was not taken and why. An
# evaluation that cannot exist because a third party is unreachable is a dependency nobody
# agreed to.
#
# The model is named on every round it marks (`zz.eval.judge_model` records `typesafe/<model>`),
# so a change of judge reads like a change of rubric version instead of silently redefining what
# every earlier score meant.
TYPESAFE_API_KEY=
TYPESAFE_MODEL=jev-latest
# A self-hosted endpoint speaking the same contract. Empty means the public service.
TYPESAFE_BASE_URL=
# How long one request may take before the round reports the subject skipped and moves on. A
# whole ruler rides in ONE request — the service evaluates its questions in parallel — so this
# is per subject and not per dimension.
TYPESAFE_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# The most of an INITIATIVE PAIR a judge is shown, in characters, split evenly between the two
# ends. A whole pair of real documents overruns a judgement service's input window and scores
# NOTHING — losing the subject rather than shortening it — and truncating the concatenation
# from the end would feed the whole beginning and none of the conclusion, which is the one
# comparison that subject exists to make. A cut is announced in the text the judge reads.
#
# 70000 is DERIVED, not chosen for safety. Measured against the live service: 32,275 input
# tokens accepted and ~36,400 refused, so the ceiling is 32768; real documents run 3.01
# characters per token, not the ~5 a synthetic probe suggests. 70000 clears the ceiling even at
# a pathological 2.5 chars/token with room for the ruler's own text. It was 24000, which cut
# 210,702 characters across six real initiatives and was never measured against anything.
ZZ_JUDGE_PAIR_CAP=70000

# HOW LONG THE PLATFORM DATABASE IS ALLOWED TO KEEP A CALLER WAITING.
#
# The pools are small — four connections in zz-core, six in the gateway — so an unbounded wait
# is not a slow request, it is an outage with a green health check: four hung statements and
# zz-core has no connections left, while /health keeps answering 200 because it touches no
# database.
#
# ZZ_DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS is server-side and is what actually releases the connection. It
# applies to zz-core only. The gateway deliberately has none, because that pool runs the
# migrations and a migration that rewrites a table takes as long as it takes — aborting one
# partway is worse than the pile-up it would prevent.
#
# 30s is far above anything measured here; it can only fire on something already wrong.
ZZ_DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# How long a caller waits for a free connection before being refused. Both services.
ZZ_DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# Connections per service. zz-core defaults to 4 and the gateway to 6; this overrides both.
ZZ_DB_POOL_MAX=


# The platform database's password. No default: the one that used to be here was
# `change-me`, documented forty lines below as "set on a real deployment", which is a
# password shipped to every installation that reads the required section and stops. The
# database holds the team registry, every decision and the whole audit record, and
# POSTGRES_BIND exists precisely so an operator can put it on a tailnet.
#   openssl rand -hex 16
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# The compose project, which decides what every VOLUME on this host is named:
# <project>_zz-artifacts, <project>_cred-data, and so on. Compose reads it from this file;
# so does deploy/backup.sh, because the volume names are the one thing compose offers no
# other way to ask for. Unset means the directory name, which is `deploy` — and that is a
# perfectly good answer, so leave it alone unless you have a reason.
#
# The reason it is written down at all: set it here and the volumes are renamed, and
# anything that reconstructs a volume name by hand is then pointing at a name that does not
# exist. That cost this deployment four nights of silent backup loss in August 2026.
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=

# ── what is exposed, and to whom ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One service may be published beyond loopback and it is the gateway, which authenticates
# every request itself. Everything else binds to 127.0.0.1 — reach it through an SSH
# tunnel, or bind it to a tailnet address here.
#
# These were ONE variable once. Widening the gateway's reach therefore published zz-core
# beside it, and zz-core answers tools/list and read_file to anyone its port can reach.

# GATEWAY_BIND=127.0.0.1      the gateway. Caddy on the same host reaches 127.0.0.1 fine
# INTERNAL_BIND=127.0.0.1     zz-core and the block services
# POSTGRES_BIND=127.0.0.1     the platform database

# ── building blocks ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This read "── the agents ──" until 2026-08-30. It was accurate when OWU_BASE_MODEL sat
# here; that knob became PLATFORM_BASE_MODEL and moved to the required section above, and
# the heading stayed over a section that has only ever described blocks since.

# Building blocks this deployment proxies, as {"name": {"url": ..., "header": ...}}.
# LEAVE IT UNSET unless you are adding blocks: unset means the gateway's own registry
# (services/gateway/src/blocks.ts), which is EMPTY — this release ships no block, so
# every /p/<block>/mcp answers "no such block" until you set this. Setting it REPLACES
# the registry rather than adding to it, so
# `PLATFORMS={}` is a deployment with no blocks at all and every /p/<block>/mcp
# answering 404. That was a real outage here, which is why blank is now read as unset.
# A block's per-person credentials are NOT here — each person stores their own through
# the platform, and a block collapses to an onboarding prompt for anyone who has not.
# PLATFORMS={"casebox":{"url":"https://example/mcp","header":"X-API-Key","name":"Example"}}

# ── ports ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# CRED_PROXY_PORT=8764
# ZZ_CORE_PORT=8763
# POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# ── databases and internal addresses ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# All default to the Postgres this compose starts and the service names on its network.
# We run them, so no installer can supply a better value than we already know.

# PLATFORM_DB_URL=            the platform schema; defaults to the compose Postgres
# TEAM_DB_URL=                the team registry — who is on which team, which zz-core reads
#                             to decide whose store a document lands in, and which the
#                             gateway falls back to when PLATFORM_DB_URL is unset. Both
#                             point at the same Postgres by default. POSTGRES_PASSWORD is
#                             required above; set TEAM_DB_URL and it is not consulted.
# CORE_MCP_URL=http://zz-core:8000/mcp
# EVAL_MCP_URL=http://zz-core:8000/eval-mcp
#                             the evaluation door. The same zz-core process as above, on a
#                             second MCP mount — the gateway publishes it as /eval/mcp and
#                             the ten plugin_* tools are served there rather than to every
#                             account. Two variables rather than one because the day those
#                             two doors stop being one process, that is a deployment change
#                             and not a code change.
#
# The two stand-in blocks read these. Their defaults are the compose network, so leave them
# alone unless this deployment reaches the web views from somewhere else.
#                                            {"name": {"url": ..., "headers": {...},
#                                                      "envelope": "bare"|"wrapped"}}
#
# An unconfigured peer is stubbed in SIMULATE mode and is an ERROR in test and execute —
# this said "simulated, not an error" without that distinction, which reads as "empty is
# always fine". It is not: a scenario that bridges bookings into the case platform needs a
# peer for it, and without one the build stops dead. That is the correct behaviour (a mock
# inventing results for a service nobody configured would let a workflow verify against
# nothing), but it has to be stated.
#
# `envelope` must match what the peer really returns, or a workflow passes its rehearsal and
# fails live. A block may answer with the payload wrapped in an envelope of its own.
#
# Reach a block through the gateway rather than embedding its key here: the proxy injects
# the caller's own credential, which is the whole point of it.
#
#                     "headers":{"Authorization":"Bearer <a platform token>"},
#                     "envelope":"wrapped"}}

# ── who may assert an identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The forwarded-header path lets a caller say who they are with no secret, so both of these
# are checked by resolving the hostname and matching the peer's address. Change them only
# if you rename the services.

# TRUSTED_FORWARD_HOST is EMPTY by default, and should stay that way. It names a container
# permitted to assert who a caller is with no secret at all — necessary when the browser had
# no token to send, and not since. Every client now carries its owner's PAT. Set it only for
# a front end that genuinely authenticates people itself and cannot carry their token, and
# understand what it grants: whoever reaches the gateway from that container may claim to be
# anyone.
# TRUSTED_FORWARD_HOST=
# TRUSTED_PEERS=cred-proxy               zz-core answers only these

# ── rarely touched ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# Pin a different image than the one this compose ships with. The release process writes
# the versions into docker-compose.yml; these override for a rollback or a test.
# ZZ_IMAGE=ghcr.io/zhixuan312/zz-stack
# ZZ_VERSION=

# WRITTEN BY THE RELEASE, not by you. `scripts/release.ts` records the version this host
# was on before the switch, and `--rollback` reads it — which is what makes a rollback work
# even when the release died halfway. It appears in your .env after the first release; leave
# it alone. Nothing in the compose file reads it.
# ZZ_PREVIOUS_VERSION=

# The platform database's own role and name. The database is ours; nothing else keeps
# anything in it.
# POSTGRES_USER=zz
# POSTGRES_DB=zz


# ── read from a checkout, not from a deployment ──────────────────────────────

# Where the shelf lives. `/catalog` is where it ships inside the image, and a deployment
# should leave this alone — it exists so a CHECKOUT can point the packaging code at its own
# catalog/ directory, which is what lets the release gate build a real client package and
# look at it instead of grepping the source that builds one. Commented out like every other
# optional knob: it was the one uncommented empty assignment in this file, so `cp
# .env.example .env` put it in the operator's environment set to nothing.
# ZZ_CATALOG_DIR=

# The platform's OWN skills — zz-platform and the rest. A tree beside the catalog rather
# than inside it, because they belong to no flow and are true wherever this runs. `/skills`
# is where they ship in the image, and a deployment should leave this alone; it exists for
# the same reason as the line above, so a checkout can point the packaging code at its own
# skills/ directory and the gate can build a real package with them in it.
# ZZ_SKILLS_DIR=

# The `zz` plugin's own eval cases — the suite `claude plugin eval` runs against the one
# plugin everybody installs. Beside its skills for the same reason they are beside the
# catalog: `zz` is generated per caller rather than read from `catalog/`, so its suite has
# nowhere else to live. `/evals` in the image; the override exists so a checkout and the
# gate can point at their own evals/ directory.
# ZZ_EVALS_DIR=

# ── Delegated access to a building block ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A person signs in to the block THEMSELVES and grants what they choose; the platform then
# calls that block as them. The block's audit log names the person, their own limits apply,
# and nobody has to create or paste a key.
#
# Per block, because two blocks are two registrations — sharing one client would ask each to
# trust the other's redirect. Leave a block's pair unset and it simply keeps using a stored
# key; nothing breaks.
#
# The callback you register with each provider is exactly:
#     ${GATEWAY_PUBLIC_URL}/oauth/<block>/callback
# matched byte for byte at their end, so GATEWAY_PUBLIC_URL must be right before you ask.
#
# NO PAIR IS LISTED HERE, because this deployment ships no block. Six keys were, for three
# mock blocks that moved to their own repository, so an operator read six knobs that nothing
# could ever use and could not tell them from live ones. Their names are deliberately not
# written out: the gate finds a documented key by matching the literal, so naming a dead one
# here makes it look live again — which is the exact confusion this paragraph exists to end.
#
# ADDING ONE IS THREE EDITS, and each name is written out literally in all three because the
# gate checks documented-against-read by finding the literal:
#     1. `<BLOCK>_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=` and `_SECRET=` here (and `_SCOPE=` if the block needs one)
#     2. the same names in deploy/docker-compose.yml's `environment:` — that list is explicit,
#        so a name it does not carry never reaches the container
#     3. the same names in the two client maps at the top of
#        services/gateway/src/block-oauth.ts
#
# Until then a block uses a stored key through cred-proxy, and `block_connect` refuses by name
# rather than failing silently.

# ── The admin console: passkey sign-in ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The console (a separate app, served beside this gateway) shows every team's work at once,
# and its only door is a passkey. There is nothing to register with a provider and no secret
# here to rotate — the one setting is where a browser reaches the console.
#
# THIS IS THE RELYING PARTY. Its hostname becomes the RP ID and its origin the expected
# origin, both derived from this single value so the two cannot be spelled apart. Unset, the
# gateway answers 503 and says so rather than guessing an origin from the Host header — the
# one value worth forging is exactly the one that must not come from the request.
#
# A CREDENTIAL IS BOUND TO THE HOSTNAME IN HERE. Change it and every registered passkey stops
# verifying at once, and everybody re-enrols with a fresh link from
# `./deploy/issue-enrolment.sh`. Worth knowing before you move a host, not after.
#
# NOBODY REGISTERS THEMSELVES. A principal is created first (`person_add`), then a superadmin
# mints a one-time enrolment link for it. The first one comes from the host, because before
# anyone has a passkey there is no superadmin session to mint it:
#     ./deploy/issue-enrolment.sh someone@example.com
CONSOLE_PUBLIC_URL=

